// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: a bare member LITERAL handed to a `std:array` export's `needle: T`
// over a literal-union array. `vl check` exits 0 with no diagnostic at any severity and is
// the ONLY command that misses it — `vl build` writes the `.wasm` and then exits 1 saying it
// cannot instantiate (the invalid file is left on disk deliberately), `vl check --codegen`
// exits 1, and `--no-validate` is what restores the write-and-bless path. The engine refuses
// the module at load —
//
//   type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)
//
// FOUR LINES, IMPORTING ONLY STD, AND PLAIN `vl check` IS THE ONLY COMMAND THAT MISSES IT.
// (`vl build`, `vl run` and `vl check --codegen` all catch it; the hazard is that `vl check`
// is what an editor runs.) That is the whole reason this file exists: `std/array.vl`'s reach section
// had just been rewritten to say a literal-union ELEMENT is admitted with no carve-out at
// all, and this program is the counter-example a std review found four lines from the claim.
// It is over-promise number SEVEN in that header's ledger, and the first one on the
// DISHONEST failure kind.
//
// THE MECHANISM is the ARGUMENT boundary of a monomorphized instance, the one member of the
// family that the element READ (#1910), the element STORE (#1917) and the RESULT list all
// left alone. `indexOf<T>(self: T[], needle: T)` instantiated at `T := K` has an atom-repped
// parameter — a literal union's members are PRESERVED at element position, so `K` is the
// interned i32 id — while the argument `"b"` is a bare `StrLit` in an ordinary expression
// position, where `ctxKeepsLitUnion` SOFTENS to a string ref. The string ref goes into the
// i32 parameter slot.
//
// THE ATOM REP IS THE TRIGGER, so the hazard scope is the COMPILED PROGRAM, not the spelling
// at the call site. Measured, every cell run:
//
//   xs: K[] (a declared alias)                        xs.indexOf("b")   → invalid module
//   xs: ("a"|"b")[] inline, no alias in the program    xs.indexOf("b")   → runs, `1`
//   xs: ("a"|"b")[] inline + an UNUSED `type Unrelated = "a"|"b"`        → invalid module
//   xs: ("file"|"dir")[] + `import { pathKind } from "std:fs"`           → invalid module
//   xs: K[], the needle BOUND first (`const needle: K = "b"`)            → runs, `1`
//   ns: N[] where `type N = 1 | 2`, `ns.count(2)`                        → runs, `1`
//   a hand-written `find<T>(self: T[], needle: T)` importing nothing     → invalid module
//   a NON-generic `function f(n: K)` called `f("b")`                     → runs, `1`
//
// So it is not a std bug and it is not about the alias you wrote — `std:fs` exports
// `FileKind = "file" | "dir"`, which is enough to make a `("file" | "dir")[]` fail by IMPORT
// ALONE, exactly as the same file's ordering carve-out records for its own hazard.
//
// IT REACHES THE FOUR EXPORTS THAT TAKE A `needle: T` and no others, measured export by
// export: `indexOf`, `lastIndexOf`, `includes`, `count` all fail; `reverse`, `concat` and
// `sorted` run, because none of them takes a bare `T`.
//
// REMEDY, measured in a module that DOES declare the alias: BIND THE NEEDLE FIRST. A
// `const needle: K = "b"` is an atom-repped BINDING, so it meets the atom-repped parameter
// and the call runs.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, fold a running row into `array-mapindexed-litunion-result.vl`'s neighbourhood,
// and re-probe `std/array.vl`'s ADMITTED paragraph — that paragraph is what this row exists
// to keep honest.
import { indexOf } from "std:array"

type K = "a" | "b"

const xs: K[] = ["a", "b"]
print(xs.indexOf("b"))
